toperator91.pp	gap: the duplicate-conversion check keys on the result type KIND, so String[80], String[90] and ShortString are one result type and the second Explicit/Implicit declaration is refused (pasparser_call.inc:492 via OpConvResultMatches, which treats all frozen kinds as equal ON PURPOSE -- toperator93 needed that). TWO HALVES, COSTED SEPARATELY 2026-09-06. The DECLARATION half needs a ProcRetStrCap column, the return-side twin of ProcParamStrCap (which now exists, ec7aee581) -- contained. The USE-SITE half is measured fpc 3.2.2 behaviour and is idiosyncratic: running the row under fpc prints `TString80 Explicit`, `TString90 Explicit`, then `ShortString Implicit` SIX TIMES -- so only the two explicit casts to the exactly-named types reach their own operators, and every implicit assignment picks the generic ShortString one INCLUDING `s80 := t` where a TString80 Implicit is declared and in scope. Those two operators are declared and never called. Reproducing that ranking is emulating a rule no real program can be relying on; the declaration half is the part with value.
